WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:05.740
When Apple engineers pitched Steve Jobs on
turning the iPod into an iPhone, he responded,

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.549
“Why the f*&amp;! would we want to do that?”

00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:10.240
“That is the dumbest idea I’ve ever heard.”

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.780
reported Adam Grant in his book Think Again.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:18.520
Jobs feared creating a phone would undermine
the iPod, which sold 20 million units in 2005

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.939
and accounted for nearly half of Apple’s
revenue that year.

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:26.190
Besides, Jobs thought a phone would only appeal
to the “pocket-protector crowd”.

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:31.570
He was also concerned that mobile phone carriers
would dictate the iPhone’s design and features

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:34.150
and force Apple to make a subpar product.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:37.600
But those who wanted an Apple phone refused
to give up.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.590
Tony Fadell who headed the team that built
the iPod, told Adam Grant that in order to

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:46.699
convince Jobs, people “had to work as a
group, not simply in one meeting but possibly

00:00:46.699 --> 00:00:51.500
over weeks, to get him to change his mind
or to see things from another angle.”

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.420
Contrary to popular belief, Steve Jobs wasn’t
a success solely because he bent the world

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:57.660
to his vision.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.230
Much of his success had to do with a willingness
to change his mind.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.619
And his team knew how to get him to do that.

00:01:04.619 --> 00:01:08.790
Before the iPhone, just about everyone at
Apple thought cell phones sucked.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:12.290
Jobs was known to smash his to bits when his
calls dropped.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:16.261
But pitching to Jobs wasn’t as simple as
saying: Wouldn’t it be awesome if Apple

00:01:16.261 --> 00:01:18.680
built a phone everyone loved using?

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.310
It involved strategically chipping away at
his resistance.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:27.430
Two years before presenting the iPhone concept
to Jobs, a small group of Apple’s designers

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:31.479
and engineers experimented with new ways of
interfacing with machines.

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:36.670
They stumbled upon a small Delaware company,
Fingerworks, co-founded by a Ph.D. student

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:41.900
and his professor, who built a touchpad that
interacted directly with the screen.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:46.619
Apple would eventually acquire FingerWorks
and its patents related to finger gestures.

00:01:46.619 --> 00:01:51.720
The Apple team aspired to expand beyond a
touchpad to a full touchscreen, perhaps for

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.540
a device like a tablet.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:57.969
Apple designer Imran Chaudhri explained to
Brian Merchant in his book, the One Device,

00:01:57.969 --> 00:02:02.500
“...we want to be able to move things on
the screen like a piece of paper on the table.”

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:07.570
They worked in stealth mode, worried that
if Jobs found out about it and didn’t like

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:10.060
it, the whole thing could get quashed.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:14.900
After showing the multi-touch demo to Apple’s
chief designer Jony Ive who had Jobs’s ear.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.330
Ive told the team, “This is going to change
everything.”

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:23.110
But he was careful to show it to Jobs privately
because of his tendency to dismiss ideas out

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:24.110
of hand.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:29.470
Ive explained to Walter Isaacson in his biography
on Jobs: “He might say, ‘This is sh@!,’

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:30.590
and snuff the idea.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:35.500
I feel that ideas are very fragile, so you
have to be tender when they are in development.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:40.970
I realized that if he pissed on this, it would
be so sad because I knew it was so important.”

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:45.780
After mulling it over for a few days, he agreed
touch was the future.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.680
But he was still not sold on a phone.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:54.190
Yet, other phones on the market began to threaten
the iPod with their ability to play music,

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:56.370
like Nokia’s music phones.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:01.050
Apple then decided to partner with Motorola
to incorporate the iPod into a new Motorola

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.050
cell phone.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.050
It’s a pretty cool phone.

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:08.860
Well, actually, the ROKR was neither minimalistic
like the iPod nor slim like Motorola’s popular

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:09.860
RAZR.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:14.459
It was such a disaster that Wired magazine
lamented on its cover: “You call this the

00:03:14.459 --> 00:03:16.610
phone of the future?”

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:21.290
Putting iPod features into a Motorola phone
“...just seemed ass-backwards to me.”

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:24.820
recalled Apple senior executive Mike Bell
in Merchant’s book.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:29.090
Bell was one of those who had lobbied Jobs
to build an Apple phone for months.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:32.340
In November 2004, he emailed Jobs late at
night:

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.909
Steve, I know you don’t want to do a phone,
but here’s why we should do it: Jony Ive

00:03:36.909 --> 00:03:40.709
has some really cool designs for future iPods
that no one has seen.

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:45.000
We ought to take one of those, put some Apple
software around it, and make a phone out if

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.519
ourselves instead of putting our stuff on
other people’s phones.

00:03:48.519 --> 00:03:51.470
Jobs called him, and they argued for hours.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:55.810
Bell pointed out it was only a matter of time
before computers, music players, and cell

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:57.230
phones converged.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:01.730
The team believed that if they made the product
compelling enough, they could influence mobile

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:07.630
carriers to adopt Apple's terms for design,
software, and distribution.

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:09.810
Finally, Jobs relented, telling Bell:

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.370
“Okay, I think we should go do it.”

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:14.930
They considered two different versions of
the iPhone.

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:19.769
The first one was modeled after the iPod but
the scroll wheel made it tough to dial phone

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:20.769
numbers.

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:25.370
Jobs humorously added an outdated rotary dial
to the mock-up to show the limitations of

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:26.729
that design.

00:04:26.729 --> 00:04:31.199
The second option was multitouch, which was
more akin to a mini-computer.

00:04:31.199 --> 00:04:34.480
Jobs was convinced touch was the way forward.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.240
Selecting the material for the iPhone’s
touchscreen was another major hurdle.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:43.750
Just four months before Jobs planned to reveal
the iPhone to the world, he decided to switch

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:49.770
the screen from plastic to glass because plastic
was too easily scratched.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:52.960
Engineers were in a frenzy because they knew
they had to find a glass replacement that

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:57.900
would pass the one-meter drop test, and so
far, all the prototypes had failed.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:03.320
A friend of Jobs suggested he reach out to
Wendel Weeks, the CEO of Corning, an American

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:08.389
glassware company that had once prototyped
a strong, scratch-proof product dubbed “gorilla

00:05:08.389 --> 00:05:10.930
glass” but didn’t have a market for it.

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:15.010
When they met, Jobs doubted the glass was
good enough and started lecturing Weeks on

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:16.530
how glass was made.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:21.150
Weeks boldly interjected, as described in
Issaacson’s book: “Can you shut up, and

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.280
let me teach you some science?”

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.800
In the end, Jobs was convinced.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.020
But then Weeks said his company didn’t have
the capacity to make the gorilla glass in

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:33.270
its factories, to which Jobs said: “Don’t
be afraid.

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.009
Yes, you can do it.

00:05:35.009 --> 00:05:36.810
Get your mind around it.

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:37.870
You can do it.”

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:39.669
And Corning did do it.

00:05:39.669 --> 00:05:43.150
Weeks told Isaacson: “We did it in under
six months.

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:46.540
We produced a glass that had never been made.”

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:50.949
The day the iPhone came out, Jobs sent Weeks
a message that’s now framed in his office:

00:05:50.949 --> 00:05:54.500
“We couldn’t have done it without you.”

00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:00.250
And we are calling it iPhone.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:07.099
Today, Apple is going to reinvent the phone.

00:06:07.099 --> 00:06:13.229
When Jobs unveiled the iPhone to the world
in January 2007, it lacked a key feature.

00:06:13.229 --> 00:06:16.490
The original iPhone had no external apps.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:19.220
It was limited to Apple’s pre-installed
apps.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:20.669
There was no App Store.

00:06:20.669 --> 00:06:25.660
Jobs was worried that a poorly written third-party
app would cause the iPhone to crash, especially

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:29.270
in critical situations where someone might
need to call 911.

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:34.130
However, Scott Forstall, head of the iPhone’s
software division, saw the potential of a

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:36.229
rich app ecosystem.

00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:40.240
He told Jobs that they could isolate and manage
any security risks.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:44.729
The push for third-party apps wasn’t just
internal; developers were advocating for an

00:06:44.729 --> 00:06:45.729
open platform.

00:06:45.729 --> 00:06:47.110
And they got it.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:49.669
What we call the App Store

00:06:49.669 --> 00:06:53.800
Apple officially launched the App Store in
the summer of 2008.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:58.639
Allowing third-party apps immediately pushed
iPhone sales through the roof.

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:03.330
Four years after its launch, the iPhone made
up nearly half of Apple’s total revenue.

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:08.069
And it still accounts for half of Apple’s
revenue today!

00:07:08.069 --> 00:07:13.789
With well over 2 billion iPhones sold, tech
industry analyst and Apple expert Horace Dediu

00:07:13.789 --> 00:07:17.190
put the stunning success of the iPhone this
way:

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:22.440
The iPhone is not only the best selling mobile
phone but also the best selling music player,

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:27.080
the best selling camera, the best selling
video screen and the best selling computer

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:28.550
of all time.

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:33.400
It is, quite simply, the best selling product
of all time.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:37.610
All because Steve Jobs was willing to change
his mind.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:42.520
Steve Jobs changed his mind about building
the iPhone because he thought like a scientist.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:47.770
He had the flexibility to reconsider his views
when presented with compelling arguments.

